#Day09 - SLAM(Simultaneous Localization and Mapping)

What is SLAM?
--SLAM(Simulataneous Localization and Mapping) is the core technology AR and VR deivces use to map unknown surroundings and track user 
positin in real time.
--It handles Inside-out tracking(as I documented before),surface detection, and spatial alignment in real time without external base.

How does SLAM work in Immersive Tech?
--Sensing: Cameras and inertial measurement units (IMUs) gather raw visual and motion data from the physical room.
--Localization: The system calculates where the headset or phone is located and how it moves relative to the room.
--Mapping: It builds a 3D point cloud or structural mesh of walls, floors, and objects so virtual items stay fixed in place.

Limitations--
--Poor lighting.
--Blank walls with few visual features.
--Reflective surfaces.
--Fast movement can reduce tracking quality.

Real World Example--
--Meta Quest 3 & Apple Vision Pro: These headsets use four or more built-in cameras to continuously scan your living room.
--SLAM processes this data to track your head movement (inside-out tracking) and map your furniture so you do not crash into walls.

Conclusion--
--Today I learnt that SLAM is one of the core technologies behind modern AR and VR systems. It allows a headset to understand its 
surroundings while simultaneously tracking its own position in realtime. This enales features like inside-out-tracking, surface 
detection and accurate placement of virtual things possible without external base.
